  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rainfall tonight then becoming more scattered\nThursday and Friday\n\nLow pressure over the upper Midwest will occlude by Wed \nevening. A front moves slowly east toward the coalfields of KY \nthis evening while southeast to south flow increases into the \nsouthern Appalachians. Models are still favoring showers \ndeveloping as early as mid afternoon in the NC mountains then a\nbroken line of showers moves in from WV/KY. As far as \nthunderstorms, could see enough instability for a few in the far\nsouthwest VA area toward Marion/Chilhowie this evening. Better \nsupport for thunderstorms will reside further southwest into \nthe TN Valley and Smokys. The best chance of rain will be in the\nmountains to Blue Ridge then more scattered in nature east of \nLynchburg and Danville tonight.\n\nA brief lull then occurs on Thursday, despite the front still \nremaining off to the west, due to drier air across the area. A \nslight chance of showers remains possible, before another \ndisturbance dives down around the upper-level trough and interacts \nwith the cold front over our area Thursday night into Friday \nmorning. Models have shifted the better rainfall just east of us\nThu night into Friday but still think enough lift with the low \npressure to the south to have likely pops in the Piedmont. \n\nRain totals mainly vary from a quarter to half inch, with less\nover the foothills of VA, an more across the NC mountains and \nportions of WV.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Unseasonably warm temperatures continue through \nFriday, returning to near normal this weekend. \n\nAs high pressure continues to shift offshore into the Atlantic, \nsoutherly flow will return to the Mid-Atlantic, causing temperatures \nto be well above normal for this time of year. High temperatures \nthrough the end of the week will be in the upper 60s for the \nmountains, and mid to upper 70s in the Piedmont. A few spots could \neven reach 80 degrees Wednesday and Thursday, which is about 15 \ndegrees above normal. Behind the cold front, temperatures \"cool\" \nback off to only slightly above normal, with highs mainly in the \n60s, with upper 50s for the highest elevations. \n\nOvernight lows will also be very mild through the end of the week, \nin the 50s/60s Wednesday night. By Saturday night, overnight lows \nfall into the 30s area-wide, which is around normal. This continues \nthrough the middle of next week.",
